<BaseLayout
  title="Oreslang Paradigms — actors, functional data flow, hot loading, AOT + JIT"
  description="Oreslang combines actor isolation, functional programming, data-oriented programming, hot-loaded actor generations, AOT/JIT/hybrid compilation, and contracts for static architecture."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Paradigms</p>
    <h1>Use the model that fits the work.</h1>
    <p>
      Oreslang is multi-paradigm by design. Concurrency does not need to look like data processing;
      hot deployment does not need to look like mutation; and static architecture does not need to
      erase the distinction between modules, classes, and actors.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <div class="prose">
      <h3>Actor-oriented concurrency</h3>
      <p>
        Actors are the primary concurrency boundary. Shared actors can cooperate where sharing is
        explicitly allowed; isolated and untrusted actors can run with stronger memory confinement
        and capability restrictions. Graal isolate-backed execution gives the runtime another
        boundary for workloads that need stronger separation without forcing the application into a
        different messaging model.
      </p>
      <p>
        The important abstraction is still the typed actor reference. The runtime decides whether
        the implementation executes in shared memory, confined memory, a Graal isolate, a dedicated
        execution pool, or eventually another process or machine.
      </p>

      <h3>Functional programming</h3>
      <p>
        Oreslang treats functions as first-class values and supports functional data flow directly
        rather than making it a library-only convention. Native <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>,
        and <code>reduce</code> pair naturally with rx-ores style pipelines, futures, channels, and
        immutable values.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tail-call optimization belongs in this model too: recursive functional code should not have
        to choose between expressive structure and avoidable stack growth when a tail call can be
        compiled as iteration.
      </p>

      <div class="paradigm-flow" aria-label="Functional composition example">
        <span>source</span><b>→</b><span>map</span><b>→</b><span>filter</span><b>→</b><span>reduce</span><b>→</b><span>actor</span>
      </div>

      <h3>Data-oriented programming</h3>
      <p>
        Data-oriented programming is about shaping runtime data for the hardware that will process it,
        not merely making types easier for a compiler to understand. Object-oriented APIs can still be
        useful at system boundaries, but hot paths should be free to organize data around access
        patterns, locality, bandwidth, and parallel execution instead of around object graphs.
      </p>
      <p>
        On CPUs, that means thinking about cache lines, prefetching, branch behavior, pointer chasing,
        contiguous storage, SIMD/vectorization, and choosing structures such as array-of-structs versus
        struct-of-arrays based on how the data is actually traversed. The representation should help the
        CPU spend more time operating on useful data and less time waiting on memory.
      </p>
      <p>
        On GPUs, the same idea becomes even more explicit: use compact buffers, predictable element
        widths, coalesced access patterns, large batches, and layouts that map efficiently onto GPU
        threads. Host-to-device copies and format conversion are part of the cost model, so data should
        be organized to minimize unnecessary transfers and reshaping between CPU and GPU work.
      </p>
      <p>
        Oreslang's static shapes, tuples, structs, actor boundaries, GPU routines, and typed execution
        model can give the compiler and JIT more information, but that is a secondary advantage. The
        primary goal is to let developers and the runtime choose data representations that execute well
        on real CPUs and GPUs while preserving the language's safety guarantees.
      </p>

      <h3>Hot-loading as a deployment primitive</h3>
      <p>
        Hot loading is useful during development, but the more interesting case is production.
        Instead of mutating a live actor's implementation in place, the runtime can load a new code
        image and start a new isolated actor generation inside a confined memory boundary. New work
        can move to the new generation while the old generation drains, finishes, or is rolled back.
      </p>
      <div class="generation-strip">
        <span>actor v1</span>
        <b>drain</b>
        <span>confined boundary</span>
        <b>load</b>
        <span>actor v2</span>
        <b>route new work</b>
      </div>
      <p>
        That gives development hot reload and production hot deployment a common conceptual model:
        load new code, preserve isolation, make the transition explicit, and avoid unsafe ambient
        mutation of running state.
      </p>

      <h3>AOT, JIT, and hybrid execution</h3>
      <div class="compile-grid paradigm-compile-grid">
        <article class="compile-card">
          <span>AOT</span>
          <h4>Predictable before startup</h4>
          <p>Resolve types, ownership, imports, closed shapes, and as much dispatch as possible before deployment. Good for startup-sensitive FaaS, mobile, and native packaging.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="compile-card">
          <span>JIT</span>
          <h4>Optimize what becomes hot</h4>
          <p>Profile real execution and specialize hot actor handlers, functional pipelines, and recurring data shapes when a runtime JIT is available.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="compile-card">
          <span>HYBRID</span>
          <h4>Start compiled, then specialize</h4>
          <p>Boot from ahead-of-time compiled code and selectively tier hot regions upward. Environments without a JIT can remain AOT-only without changing source semantics.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p>
        The goal is not to make developers choose a different language subset for each mode. The
        same typed program should carry enough information for AOT compilation, JIT specialization,
        or a hybrid runtime to make sensible decisions.
      </p>

      <h3>Contracts for static architecture</h3>
      <p>
        Oreslang keeps modules and classes distinct, so their contracts should remain distinct too.
        Module contracts describe the required static shape of a module. Static-member contracts for
        classes describe the required class-level API without pretending instance interfaces cover
        static members.
      </p>
      <p>
        This gives larger systems a way to enforce plugin surfaces, provider modules, factories,
        registries, class-level constructors, and other static architecture at compile time while
        preserving normal lexical behavior inside modules and classes.
      </p>

      <div class="paradigm-summary">
        <article>
          <span>Concurrency</span>
          <strong>actors + isolation</strong>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Transformation</span>
